#passage(("Me personally, I completely rejected it wholesale. When I say rejected wholesale, that doesn't mean animals and insects can't adapt to their environment. Of course they can! Adapting to your environment is not the same as changing from one species to another. Now, I'm talking about changing from one species to another. To say animals adapt to their environment: yes! They have behavioral adaptment; I don't have any issues with that. I see that as a sign of God's creation. So evolution can have true things, which does not conflict with my belief anyway. So it's truth mixed with falsehood. Now, what do I completely reject? I reject wholesale that humans have evolved from different species, and that species are transitioning from one to another, coming from either one origin or five, whichever way you want to take the [Third Way] of Evolution or you don't want to. Irrelevant! I reject this idea.", "Now, on the other hand, I got to separate between me and what Islam says. I could have certain positions that are not necessarily explicit in Islamic texts. But in this case, I want to just tell you what a bare minimum Muslim can believe. A Muslim cannot believe that humans evolved from different species. This is clear cut in the text (anNisa4:1). But the Muslim can say: I want to accept the animals evolved from different species, \"Guided Evolution\" they would call it, through God. That doesn't necessarily contradict the Quran. I reject it; so that's a personal view now. But you don't need to do that as a Muslim. I reject it based on my research. Other people want to accept it or reject it, that's their own worldview. It's not something that makes you a Muslim or a non-Muslim.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Is God Necessary? Intellectual Atheist Challenges The Muslim Understanding Of God! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bOPzUt3vmGs", ((1788, "29:48–30:47"), (1849, "30:49–31:42"),), id: 5045)
