#passage(("God is what we call الحقّ (He's The Truth), so whatever He says is true, whatever He says is good, whatever he says not to do is bad, by default. So Allah is the source of Right and Wrong, is the source of Morality. So at any time Allah can command something, and that something can be permissible at a specific point, and then at a different point Allah can say don't do that thing, and that thing can be immoral in that point. For example: we have different, what we call Sharias, different Legislations for different Prophets. The same beliefs about God, but different Legislations for the people. For example: at the time of Joseph you could actually prostrate to a human being, out of respect, not worship. Today as Muslims, the prophet completely prohibited us from prostrating to anything living, anything other than God. So this form of prostrating was accepted at one point, that God legislated it to be accepted, and at other point God legislated that that thing is not permissible.", "These moral aspects can change based on the Commands of God. But the question is this: how did they multiply at the time of Adam and Eve? There's nothing in the Quran that stipulates what you said about them having incest. I think that's a more Biblical idea than Islam. Islam does not go into details of how it happened. Did Eve come from Adam? She was created from his rib, not birth. She came from him, but not through Birth. The Quran stating coming from someone does not necessarily mean it's through birth. There is nothing explicit in the Quran or in the Hadith that says that this used to happen, but in the event that this might have happened, morality can change based on the Commands of God.", "Not only that: a command of God that God says don't do, sometimes you have to do by necessity. So for example: Allah says don't eat pork. If there is a necessity that I'm going to die and the only food that is there is pork, I have to eat enough to live. I'm sinning if I don't eat; I killed myself if I don't eat, because I had a chance to save my life and I didn't follow. So there are exceptions to rules as well. So Allah عزّوجلّ says in the (Quran6:119) وَقَدْ فَصَّلَ لَكُم مَّا حَرَّمَ عَلَيْكُمْ إِلَّا مَا اضْطُرِرْتُمْ إِلَيْهِ ۗ (He stipulated for you that which you cannot do except that which you have to do by necessity). So it's not as simple as do's and don'ts. The Commands in Islam and the commands in the Quran and Sunnah can have a lot of exceptions, can have a lot of different aspects and images that you need to look at as well, when you think about Morality.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Lady Skeptical About The Prophet of Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QIxWrKc_J_Y", ((489, "8:09–9:03"), (548, "9:08–10:14"), (620, "10:20–11:10"),), id: 5052)
