#passage(("\"The theory of Evolution: Fact Or Fiction.\" What is a \"fact\"? Generally, when someone says something is a fact, they say that there is NO DOUBT in that thing. We agree on it. It's a fact that water is wet. We're not going to get someone in the future who's going to come and disagree on that: \"You know what? I'm not really sure that water is wet; it could be wet sometimes and sometimes it could not be wet.\" A fact is a fact! What happens is this: in the Scientific Community, what they do is that they sell you Evolution as a fact, that there's no doubt about that. You go to Universities, anything related to biology, it's already a fact; people believe Evolution is a fact. Is it really fact, or is it the opposite of fact? Is it fiction?", "What we need to do as Muslims is very important here: we cannot be on the back foot when it comes to Evolution. What do I mean by that? Evolution is not our claim; why are we trying to disprove something which is not our claim, to begin with?! If you're moving from this perspective, you've already lost. What you need to do is to ask the other individual to demonstrate to you why this is a fact. If I'm generally talking to someone who believes in Evolution, the onus of the claim is on him. He's supposed to demonstrate to me evidence why Evolution is true. But what tends to happen in our conversations: OK! We assume Evolution is true, even though we don't agree it is, and then we start defending why we don't believe in Evolution! I say that's the biggest problem that we have today!", "The other person needs to come and give me evidence; they must demonstrate evidence to me, in front of my eyes: \"Look, this is the evidence why Evolution is true.\" And then I can maybe think about refuting the evidence that he's bringing forward, critiquing it. It's like you and me starting a conversation with an Atheist, and then him accepting the assumption, according to his worldview, that God exists, and then I ask him: disprove God! Would any Atheist ever accept that? No! So why are we doing this? This is what we need to do from now onwards. We cannot be those people who are like: \"Yeah, OK! Evolution is true, let me try to explain it.\" You're already defeated! We're defeated in a battle we never started anyway. Sometimes I do that with the existence of God, but that's a different discussion. But generally this is the idea: the person, he's got to demonstrate to us why Evolution is true.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslims Scientifically And Rationally Dismantle Evolution! Muhammed Ali & @SubboorAhmadAbbasi”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3TrwJOx-kUM", ((6, "0:06–0:56"), (102, "1:42–2:32"), (158, "2:38–4:10"),), id: 5054)
// U ML124:428-461 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("You said, when I presented you things about the truthfulness of Islam, even though you can see the clear reason there, you want to do research and investigate more. And that's what you've been doing, being skeptical. Now, you're accepting evolution. You're telling us right now that evolution is true. Have you done the same research to evolution? Or did you accept it wholesale because your community and society and your school tells you that it's true? It's OK to admit I've not researched something. That doesn't mean you're a person who's not intelligent. I do not know many things! But to say that you've researched something thoroughly when you haven't is a problem. I can easily ask you about details, and then we would know if someone did research or not. Have you researched evolution thoroughly to the extent that you're sure now? You know how the theory works, you know the mechanisms, you know the criticisms, and you know what the other side has to say, and then you're sure based on doing that: now I accept that. Because that's what you should do before you accept, but you accepted it first. So I'm showing just an inconsistency here.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Is God Necessary? Intellectual Atheist Challenges The Muslim Understanding Of God! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bOPzUt3vmGs", ((1337, "22:17–23:39"),), id: 5055)
