#passage(("God is one, but we're not God. We're the creation of God. A wave is a part of the ocean. We're not a part of the ocean. It's like your soul is a part of you. We don't separate your soul from you, but these are parts. Parts make the whole. So when you say the waves make the ocean, yes, they're parts of the whole. That's what the word part means. You don't separate, because it's a part of something. But you can take some of this water somewhere else, because it's a part. You separate it. You take that wave somewhere else. God is not divisible in that way. He's not parts where you divide him into parts.", "So if we agree, we're not a part of God. Therefore we are deficient. Basically, you agreed with me when I was trying to tell you God is perfect. We're imperfect. If you claim that our body and our soul is God, then you are claiming either a part of God, if you claim we are a part, is imperfect, or God as a whole is imperfect, if you say it's not divisible. So you fall into that problem either way.", "You have a relationship with God, cuz we're not God ourselves. We worship God. We submit to God. We humble ourselves to God. God doesn't humble himself to anyone. He doesn't submit to anyone. We submit to him. This is where the relationship comes. That doesn't take away from the idea God is one. I think this is where Satan took you from a back door, to try to convince you: if you believe the creation is separate from the creator, therefore you don't believe that God is one. No, that's not true. And it's not rational even to say it. I can believe I'm one human being, and I create something, and that thing is separate from me. Why does that stop the idea that I'm still one human being? If I create this earphone or headphone or whatever, I'm still one human being. That is just a creation of me.", "How do you know what you're saying about God? The rationality I'm bringing is based on God's himself saying what he is. The only way you can counter that is by A, disproving it's from God, or B, if you prove it's from God, then you'll have to accept that your idea about God is incorrect. Because if God himself tells me that he is X, and I come and say, I think, which I don't have proof for, but this is the conclusion I came to, that God is Z, then I have a problem. Only one of them can be true. That's why I was telling you it all has to stem from the foundation of where you get information about God from. Oneness and existence of God are things that we can reach innately and rational both. But the other idea that you're putting is not the same.", "I think this is where the problem is happening. Not that I necessarily need to accept the Greek rules of logic, but some of them are obviously true: the three laws of logic. For example, the law of non-contradiction, the law of excluded middle, the law of identity. The law of non-contradiction, for instance, is that you cannot have a square circle. It's a law of logic. So basically, any reasoning which would lead to a contradiction is flawed reasoning, because it's leading you to a contradiction. Now, there's also something called the law of identity. What is the law of identity? Is that your identity as a human being, how we define you, is different from how we define, let's say, a deer. If a deer is a human being and a human being is a deer, then you cannot have logic or rationality. It is the same thing. When I say, \"You did something,\" I'm saying the deer did something, the rock did something, everything did something. What makes separation is that we have this law of identity, where each thing has its own identity. Humans have an identity. This is how we function as human beings. Rocks have an identity. Everything has its own identity. Now, where you're getting your incorrect reasoning in is where you excluded the law of identity.", "Are you different from God or the same? Or are you God? It's a very simple question. It needs to have one of two answers, because of the law of non-contradiction. You cannot be God and not God at the same time. The minute you answer that question, you realize the contradiction of your ideology. You have one of two answers. If you would say you're not God, you've already made the duality that I was talking about, which every human being rationally agrees to: that there is humans and there is God, there is animals and there is God, there's creation and there's creator. That's why I'm saying you're deviating from your innate position. It's not the rational reasoning that a human being would reach. You're going into an incorrect conclusion there.", "It's either you say you're God or you're not God. If you say you are God, then people should be worshipping you. There's no point of us talking about anything, because you are God, you're a divine entity. When we say God, by the way, has a definition: all-knowing, all-powerful; that's what people mean by God. By definition, you must possess the attributes of God. You need to answer the contradiction: how [you're] God and at the same time you're deficient. So you are God, you're the creator, but you're weak, you're ignorant. I'm not insulting you. I'm saying all human beings are human beings. Basically, there's no other answer to that question except to say that you're not God. And if you are God and you're still claiming that you're deficient, then you're claiming God is deficient. So there's no escaping from this.", "You got into that because it's a philosophical position. It's not a rational thing that human beings go into. You don't stop people in the street and you say that everything is God. So now you're being influenced by philosophical ideology which is false, and I'm giving you the reasoning why it's false. That's not what God told you about himself, and that's not the rational conclusion that you'd arrive to as a human being on your own.", "You're confusing God with us. Law of identity: we're not the same. We all agree God is one, but we're not God. God is separate. You're not saying God is one. You're saying God is the only thing that exists, there's nothing but God. These are not the same thing. Satan got you to think that God is one equals there's nothing but God. They're not the same. \"There's nothing but God\" is an argument; \"God is one\" is an argument. I can believe God is one and there is something else that exists, but that something else is not God. That's why we say in the Shahada, which is the whole point of the Shahada: there is nothing worthy of worship, there's nothing divine except God, the only thing worthy of worship. This is the right way to understand it. But you said there's nothing at all except God. Who is saying that statement? God is saying there's nothing but God. God is pronouncing there is nothing but God, and God is worshipping God, and God is praying to God, and God is going to put God in heaven, which is God. So God is going to put God in God. There's something going on here that is not rational. This idea, there is nothing but God, is incorrect rationally.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Claims That Pantheism Is More Rational Than Monotheism! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C3BXt0zJr3o", ((2131, "35:31–36:34"), (2198, "36:38–37:00"), (2242, "37:22–38:08"), (2301, "38:21–39:13"), (2399, "39:59–41:35"), (2503, "41:43–43:10"), (2596, "43:16–44:33"), (2828, "47:08–47:36"), (2862, "47:42–49:16"),), id: 5096)
