#passage(("What you're calling to right now is something that we refer to as وحدة الأديان (Unity of Religion), and we believe that's a corrupted kind of creed; we don't agree with that. But the first thing I want to say is this: Islam doesn't say what you said. Islam says that Islam was there from the time of Adam, it was there from the beginning. It was there before Hinduism or any other religion on Earth. Islam says that Jesus, Moses, Noah, and Abraham were Prophets of God. They received the Revelation of God: that Jesus got a Gospel from the Creator. This is Revelation, but people change it later on. So we're not saying \"Prophet Muhammadﷺ received Revelation and everything else is this..\" We don't say that! We say there were previous Revelations, they've been changed, they've been corrupted, so there is Truth and Falsehood within. So Allah is sending a clarification for everyone else.", "Now a second thing I would say on what you said is: look at all of these different religions. One says that God is three in one, one says that God has millions of manifestations, and one says that God is one, does not have pictures or images, and He doesn't have different persons, it's just one Creator. Now it is impossible for you to say that all of these things are true at the same time. There is something called the Law of Non-Contradiction: I cannot have a square-circle or a married-bachelor, etc. So in order for us to have reasoning, we have to agree with something and disagree with something. If something is in existence, the opposite cannot also be in existence at the same time. So yes, there's a lot of claims. But how many of these claims have brought evidences forth to prove that what they have is from the Creator?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Should We Doubt Everything?! Muhammed Ali - Speakers Corner”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcAO_agqBFE", ((377, "6:17–7:05"), (428, "7:08–7:45"),), id: 5107)
// U ML407:606-651 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("If we just talk about what we agree upon, I say \"Yeah\", you say \"Yeah\"; I say \"No\", you say \"No\"; there's no discussion. If I want to understand Tobias, then I want to see how Tobias views the world and how I view the world, and why Tobias views the world differently to the way I do. If the world was all the same, like you're saying, it'll be a boring world. We all like the same woman, we all like the same food: what kind of a world is that! Allah says it's one of his signs: he created us in different colours with different languages so we can get to know one another (Alhujurat49:13). This is a sign of the Creator; this difference within this life is a sign of an intent and a knowledgeable Creator. So to say that that's something bad, that's something I'll never understand.", "You want to combine everything in one even though it's contradictory. That doesn't work. It will never work. Do you think you're the first person to bring liberal ideas and \"let's unite it all together in one religion\"? It doesn't work, because people understand that there are contradictions. And if there's contradictions, then one has to be right and one has to be wrong. Not everything is black and white, but to say that this building doesn't exist, and I say that this building exists: one of them is true, that's black and white. Certain things, you will have to reduce it into right or wrong. It's either you exist or you don't exist. It's either there's a God or there is no God. It's either the universe has a beginning or it doesn't have a beginning. There's no third option. \"I don't know\" is not a category.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Should We Doubt Everything?! Muhammed Ali - Speakers Corner”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcAO_agqBFE", ((1527, "25:27–26:07"), (1570, "26:10–26:51"),), id: 5108)
