#passage(("Now the last idea is this idea about advancement. Many countries are advanced from a worldly perspective. You've got other Khaliji (Gulf) countries which are also advanced, if you look at Qatar, Dubai, Emirates and all these countries. That doesn't mean that they're advanced from a spiritual perspective. They can be advanced from a worldly perspective but not a spiritual perspective. But what you're doing is this: you're looking at a very small part of history and you're making a conclusion. Muslims had an empire that lasted for over 600 years, the Ottoman Empire. They were the strongest people in the world at that time. They were the most advanced worldly, and from الآخرة (Akhira: Afterlife) perspective, they were people practising the religion. What does that prove now? It doesn't prove anything! Just because today you have a civilization in the west that tends, in history today, to have the power for a certain period of time, that does not mean now everything they believe is true.", "If you actually study and look at the outcomes of their teachings, the outcome of their morality, you'll understand why those people are the most miserable people they could be. Highest divorce rates, highest murder rates, no safety anywhere in their countries, in their lands. An Imam was stabbed to death in America just one or two days ago. The people in some parts of America steal up to $1,000 and there's no [prosecution] by law. Left and right people accusing each other of sexual harassment. The UN has a study called \"Women abused everyday, everywhere\", which is about the US and the west; the study is just about them. It's tens of thousands of women; it's one of the biggest studies done by the UN. You look at the depression rates: the biggest study that was done by Blanchflower and Oswald: the women since the 1960s until the 2000s, they were more depressed than ever, since feminism, since the advancements of the liberal period that you're talking about.", "You look at