#passage(("A very important topic, and that is Artificial Intelligence. I'm sure a lot of you have already heard about all of these new programmes such as ChatGPT, DeepSeek, Grok on Twitter, and all of these different programmes that are being used actually daily today by hundreds of millions of people, and millions of them are Muslims. So the question is: is this a good thing? Does it have a good influence on the Muslim community, that we start using and relying on these types of programmes? I started recently noticing this pattern of problematic things that are coming out, and issues, really, really big issues, that are influencing the Muslim community due to Artificial Intelligence.", "So the first thing I think I should do is to talk about what is Artificial Intelligence. Because I think some people are extremely ignorant when it comes to the identity of what is Artificial Intelligence, how does it work. And due to that, they give it a lot of authority; they rely on what it says, it's like gospel truth or something, and many of these people are Muslims, and it's a huge issue. And non-Muslims are doing the same thing. You go on Twitter, you have two people discussing and arguing, and then one of them just mentions Grok on Twitter, and then he's like: is this true? or is this false? And then he uses whatever the Artificial Intelligence says as proof or evidence, as if what has been said is true.", "So how do these programmes work? They simply work through: you design a programme, and then you input data within that programme, you input certain algorithms. So, for example, you would put an algorithm within ChatGPT that, when a question is asked, search Reddit, for example, search YouTube, search articles, search these specific websites. When a scientific question is asked, go to Science, Nature, go to this and that. So you will input this specific command within the Artificial Intelligence. Also, where you direct the Artificial Intelligence to go, the responses of the Artificial Intelligence will reflect wherever it is collecting data from. So you need to understand this is just a programme, and that programme is collecting information depending on the person who programmed it, where it's going to collect the information from. Now keep in mind, the people who are designing these programmes are not Muslims. They're non-Muslims, and they are Western people, and they have their own agendas. You look at DeepSeek, whether it's used by the Chinese, or you look at Grok: each of these people have their own agendas. And therefore, whatever that Artificial Intelligence is going to say, and answer you, and reflect, most of the time is going to be based on the views that these people hold, and the agendas that they're trying to accomplish.", "So the problem is when people don't understand the biases within these programmes, and that leads to huge issues. Many people who are experiencing doubts, some of them have become hyper sceptics. Why? Because they go to ChatGPT. Like the Quran says: (ask the people of knowledge if you don’t know) (anNahl16:43); they think it's: go ask ChatGPT if you don't know! So they go to Sheikh ChatGPT, or Sheikh Grok, or whatever, and they go ask him questions, and then they get confused. Why are they getting confused? Because that Artificial Intelligence programme is either collecting information from Reddit, or from whatever articles out there online in the English language, and then it's responding to the questions, and most of the people commenting and making posts in these websites are non-Muslims: people who hate Islam, misrepresent Islam, and put incorrect information. So when that Muslim's asking the Artificial Intelligence, he is under the influence that the Artificial Intelligence is objective, that it's not biased, it's objective, and it's going to give me the right answer, and it's done research in every book on planet Earth or something. And even in the Arab world, I've seen sadly some YouTubers are relying on ChatGPT, and they're supposed to be Muslim YouTubers. It's like: Oh! I saw a question in the comments, and I went to ChatGPT, and it told me this Hadith and that Hadith. Are we leaving people of knowledge and the scholars, and going to ChatGPT and asking it questions?", "Now, for example, you can go to ChatGPT. I was just doing that, so I can give an example. So I asked ChatGPT: based on rationality and evidence, give me a simple yes or no; in your view, does God exist? So it responded by: based strictly on rational analysis and available evidence, the answer is no; there is no conclusive evidence that God exists. It gives you an answer, a very atheistic answer. So the person who's programming this is programming it in an atheistic, naturalistic way. What's the proof of that? The proof of that is the next question I ask: What is your source for this claim? So then it says great questions, and then it starts listing what? Number one, scientific method and empiricism, when the question of God is not even a scientific question. So it's an atheistic world view. Scientism, in a way, is using science to prove or disprove the existence of God. And it says observable, testable, and repeatable. Who said that God is like atoms that you're going to put under a microscope or something? Why are you using an incorrect method? And then it says naturalism, and the burden of proof. So whose method is naturalism? Naturalism is an atheistic method. It's an atheistic ideology.", "So when you look at these programmes, you need to understand that the people programming these things are atheists. They're not Muslims. So it's going to collect data from sources which are non-Muslim sources most of the time. So as a Muslim, you cannot go to a programme like this and rely on it for Islamic questions. That is the most idiotic thing you could possibly do. For example, I ask it, is there scientific errors in the Quran? It's like, yes, there's scientific errors in the Quran. And then it went to talk about sun setting in a muddy spring. You all know all of this nonsense, which we've responded to 100 million times. But because it relies on non-Muslims, articles, Reddit, all of this nonsense, it comes back with a response reflecting whatever information it found online, or based on the person who programmes it and inserts information into it, in which case this information is not going to be supportive of our views as Muslims.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Can Muslims Really Trust Artificial Intelligence?! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7opfbjHoB1E", ((0, "0:00–0:46"), (50, "0:50–1:29"), (91, "1:31–2:50"), (173, "2:53–4:16"), (259, "4:19–5:23"), (325, "5:25–6:10"),), id: 5178)
